1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:03,220
Claude Code is one of the most powerful
coding tools out there,

2
00:00:03,240 --> 00:00:06,740
but it costs twenty dollars a month, and
the moment you hit your limit in

3
00:00:06,760 --> 00:00:09,000
the middle of a task, it just stops.

4
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:11,240
You either pay more or you wait.

5
00:00:11,260 --> 00:00:13,620
But here is what the most people don't
know about.

6
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:15,400
Claude Code itself is free.

7
00:00:15,420 --> 00:00:17,840
What you're paying for is the brain inside
it.

8
00:00:17,860 --> 00:00:21,320
And today, I am going to swap that brain
for free ones.

9
00:00:21,340 --> 00:00:24,210
Let's take a phone. The phone is just the
hardware,

10
00:00:24,340 --> 00:00:26,360
and the SIM card decides who you pay.

11
00:00:26,380 --> 00:00:30,820
Claude Code is like the phone, a free
coding assistant from Anthropic that

12
00:00:30,840 --> 00:00:34,370
lives in your terminal and can read,
write, and run code for you.

13
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:38,390
The AI model is like the SIM, the brain
that does the thinking.

14
00:00:38,420 --> 00:00:41,010
Anthropic's model costs twenty dollars a
month,

15
00:00:41,080 --> 00:00:43,580
but this phone accepts other SIMs as well.

16
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:47,600
So by the end of this video, you will have
the real Claude Code running for

17
00:00:47,620 --> 00:00:50,040
zero dollars in three different ways.

18
00:00:50,060 --> 00:00:54,620
First, completely on your own laptop, and
I'll even switch the Wi-Fi off.

19
00:00:54,640 --> 00:00:59,100
Then, free AI models in the cloud, plus
the one trap that can quietly charge

20
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:03,260
you a paid plan. And finally, several free
providers connected together,

21
00:01:03,280 --> 00:01:07,120
where I'll show you real Claude Sonnet
answering through a free account with

22
00:01:07,140 --> 00:01:10,890
the logs to prove it. And I have put
everything into a free setup kit,

23
00:01:10,980 --> 00:01:13,760
the model list, every prompt, and every
setting.

24
00:01:13,780 --> 00:01:16,040
It's the first link in the description
below.

25
00:01:16,060 --> 00:01:19,280
So stay till the end because the last
method is the best one.

26
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:21,030
Nobody paid for this video.

27
00:01:21,140 --> 00:01:24,140
I have already worked through every setup
and every trap,

28
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:27,110
so you don't have to. And I won't just
tell you it's free,

29
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:29,180
I'll show you the receipt every time.

30
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:32,080
So let's start. So method one, it's called
Ollama.

31
00:01:32,100 --> 00:01:36,160
Ollama is a free app that downloads AI
models and runs them right on your

32
00:01:36,180 --> 00:01:39,620
own laptop. Kind of like a media player,
but for AI.

33
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:44,060
The big difference from ChatGPT or Claude
is that nothing goes to the cloud.

34
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:46,940
The AI lives on your own machine, so it's
private,

35
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:49,140
and it's even works without internet.

36
00:01:49,160 --> 00:01:50,960
Installing it is super simple.

37
00:01:50,980 --> 00:01:53,520
So go to ollama.com and click Download.

38
00:01:53,540 --> 00:01:55,860
It works on Windows, Mac, and Linux.

39
00:01:55,880 --> 00:01:58,350
Then double-click the file, click through
the steps,

40
00:01:58,420 --> 00:02:01,750
and that's it. Once it's open, you'll see
three things on the left.

41
00:02:01,780 --> 00:02:05,040
Apps, this is where you connect coding
tools like Claude Code,

42
00:02:05,060 --> 00:02:09,800
Codex, and others. Chat, this is where you
talk to a model directly to test

43
00:02:09,820 --> 00:02:12,020
it. In Settings, two things matter.

44
00:02:12,040 --> 00:02:16,300
First, the cloud toggle. Ollama can also
run some models on its own servers

45
00:02:16,320 --> 00:02:19,250
in the cloud, but we want everything on
our laptop,

46
00:02:19,300 --> 00:02:22,650
so leave that off. And the second, the
most people miss,

47
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:26,560
is the context length. Context length is
the AI's short-term memory,

48
00:02:26,580 --> 00:02:30,660
how much of your conversation and your
project it can hold in its head at

49
00:02:30,680 --> 00:02:34,660
once. Claude Code reads a lot of your
files before it changes anything.

50
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:38,220
If the memory is too small, it loses track
halfway through.

51
00:02:38,240 --> 00:02:40,560
So drag this slide up to sixty-four k.

52
00:02:40,580 --> 00:02:42,540
That's the fix. Now let's test it.

53
00:02:42,560 --> 00:02:45,230
Open Chat, click the model menu at the
bottom,

54
00:02:45,380 --> 00:02:49,730
and pick a small model. I pick a small
one, Qwen 3.5 4B,

55
00:02:49,940 --> 00:02:52,150
just to warm things up, and type hi.

56
00:02:52,180 --> 00:02:55,440
The first message downloads the model, so
it takes a minute,

57
00:02:55,460 --> 00:02:56,900
but hi proves nothing.

58
00:02:56,920 --> 00:02:59,570
So watch this. I'm turning the Wi-Fi off.

59
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:01,260
See? No internet at all.

60
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:04,160
So be honest, do you think it can still
write an app?

61
00:03:04,180 --> 00:03:07,500
I type, make a simple notes app, and it's
thinking.

62
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:10,840
Thinking, and it writes the whole thing,
completely offline.

63
00:03:10,860 --> 00:03:13,980
Every single word came from a model
sitting on this laptop.

64
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:15,540
Is it pretty? No.

65
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:20,120
But a laptop with no internet just wrote
working software with no subscription,

66
00:03:20,140 --> 00:03:23,180
no usage limits, and your code never left
your computer.

67
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:26,710
And honestly, if that alone just saved you
twenty dollars a month,

68
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:29,760
hit Subscribe because it only gets better
from here.

69
00:03:29,780 --> 00:03:31,870
Now, which model should you actually run?

70
00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:34,050
Don't guess. It depends on your RAM.

71
00:03:34,100 --> 00:03:37,640
RAM is your computer's working memory, and
for local AI,

72
00:03:37,660 --> 00:03:39,900
it decides how big a model you can run.

73
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:44,000
Press Control + Shift + Escape to open
Task Manager and click the Performance

74
00:03:44,020 --> 00:03:47,790
tab. It's a pretty normal laptop, no
separate graphics card,

75
00:03:47,860 --> 00:03:50,660
just the built-in graphics and sixteen GB
of RAM.

76
00:03:50,680 --> 00:03:54,640
Roughly with eight to sixteen GB of RAM,
stick to small models,

77
00:03:54,660 --> 00:03:56,700
around four to twelve billion parameters.

78
00:03:56,720 --> 00:04:00,650
With thirty-two GB, you can go to medium,
and with sixty-four GB or more,

79
00:04:00,740 --> 00:04:03,600
you can run the big ones that feel close
to paid thing.

80
00:04:03,620 --> 00:04:07,640
The exact list I would pick for each size
is in the free setup kit.

81
00:04:07,660 --> 00:04:09,500
Here is a little trick I use too.

82
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:11,840
I ask ChatGPT, "Here is my laptop.

83
00:04:11,860 --> 00:04:15,300
Give me the best coding model for Ollama,
its exact name,

84
00:04:15,320 --> 00:04:19,240
and the latest one," because new model
comes out literally every week.

85
00:04:19,260 --> 00:04:22,970
And it suggested MiMo version two point
six, a nine B model.

86
00:04:23,140 --> 00:04:25,280
The exact prompt is in the setup kit.

87
00:04:25,300 --> 00:04:27,500
And this is where I hit my first problem.

88
00:04:27,520 --> 00:04:32,340
MiMo wasn't in Ollama's dropdown, and
Ollama only shows a handful of popular

89
00:04:32,360 --> 00:04:35,400
models there. So I tried installing it in
the terminal,

90
00:04:35,420 --> 00:04:37,710
and it told me my Ollama was too old.

91
00:04:37,760 --> 00:04:40,900
I updated it, tried again, and I was still
stuck.

92
00:04:40,920 --> 00:04:45,260
And this is exactly where a five-minute
step turned into an hour of Googling.

93
00:04:45,280 --> 00:04:48,240
So instead, I let another AI do it for me.

94
00:04:48,260 --> 00:04:52,560
It's called Antigravity. Antigravity is a
free AI coding app from Google.

95
00:04:52,580 --> 00:04:54,910
Download it from antigravity.google.

96
00:04:54,980 --> 00:04:58,610
Sign in with your Google account and open
it and give it one sentence:

97
00:04:58,660 --> 00:05:00,440
"I am running AI model locally.

98
00:05:00,460 --> 00:05:04,900
Install MiMo version two point six nine b
and set everything up so it works

99
00:05:04,920 --> 00:05:08,920
with Ollama." It will ask your permission
before running each command because

100
00:05:08,940 --> 00:05:10,620
it's running things on your machine.

101
00:05:10,640 --> 00:05:12,440
Read each step and then allow.

102
00:05:12,460 --> 00:05:16,640
A few minutes later, it says, "Installed
and tested." Close Ollama,

103
00:05:16,660 --> 00:05:20,000
open it again, and the new model is right
there in the dropdown.

104
00:05:20,020 --> 00:05:22,580
And that's going to be the rule for this
whole video.

105
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:25,750
Anything longer than one line, you don't
type it yourself,

106
00:05:25,820 --> 00:05:27,320
and AI installs it.

107
00:05:27,340 --> 00:05:30,410
And if something breaks on the way, you
just copy the error,

108
00:05:30,440 --> 00:05:33,520
paste it back to Antigravity, and say,
"Fix this." Now,

109
00:05:33,540 --> 00:05:36,560
the part you came here for, putting Claude
Code on top.

110
00:05:36,580 --> 00:05:39,400
In Ollama, go to Apps and click Claude
Code.

111
00:05:39,420 --> 00:05:42,650
See that little message? It copied the
launch command for you,

112
00:05:42,780 --> 00:05:46,550
Ollama launch Claude. Now make a folder
anywhere on your computer.

113
00:05:46,700 --> 00:05:48,370
Mine is called claude-code.

114
00:05:48,460 --> 00:05:52,280
Open it, right-click in the empty space,
and choose Open in Terminal.

115
00:05:52,300 --> 00:05:54,080
Paste the command and hit Enter.

116
00:05:54,100 --> 00:05:58,540
If the Claude Code isn't installed yet, it
offers to install it and say Yes.

117
00:05:58,560 --> 00:06:01,900
And this is the real Claude Code,
Anthropic's official app,

118
00:06:01,920 --> 00:06:06,200
not a copy. Then it asks which model to
use, and I pick my local one.

119
00:06:06,220 --> 00:06:10,720
And look at that, the real Claude Code
running on my laptop with a free local

120
00:06:10,740 --> 00:06:13,720
brain inside it. No subscription, no API
key.

121
00:06:13,740 --> 00:06:15,390
Now let's give it a real job.

122
00:06:15,420 --> 00:06:18,940
I type, "Build me a simple calculator
app." On my laptop,

123
00:06:18,960 --> 00:06:23,320
it took about thirty minutes because a
small local model is doing all the

124
00:06:23,340 --> 00:06:25,440
heavy lifting. And that's the honest
trade-off.

125
00:06:25,460 --> 00:06:27,740
Free means your laptop does the work.

126
00:06:27,760 --> 00:06:30,288
So on normal machine, you pay with your
time.

127
00:06:30,308 --> 00:06:32,188
But it finished, and it works.

128
00:06:32,208 --> 00:06:35,048
So method one is your private offline
option.

129
00:06:35,068 --> 00:06:39,188
For everyday speed, we need something
faster, so let's move to the cloud.

130
00:06:39,208 --> 00:06:41,408
So method two is called OpenRouter.

131
00:06:41,428 --> 00:06:45,848
OpenRouter is one website that gives you
access to dozens of AI models from

132
00:06:45,868 --> 00:06:49,058
different companies with one single
account and single key,

133
00:06:49,128 --> 00:06:51,328
and a lot of those models are completely
free.

134
00:06:51,348 --> 00:06:55,278
The big difference from Ollama, these
models run on their servers,

135
00:06:55,328 --> 00:06:59,388
not on your laptop, so they are much
faster, even on a basic laptop.

136
00:06:59,408 --> 00:07:02,048
Go to OpenRouter.ai and sign up.

137
00:07:02,068 --> 00:07:04,048
It's free. No credit card needed.

138
00:07:04,068 --> 00:07:06,438
Then click API keys and create key.

139
00:07:06,468 --> 00:07:11,288
A API key is basically a password that
lets an app like Claude Code use your

140
00:07:11,308 --> 00:07:13,368
OpenRouter account. Give it a name.

141
00:07:13,388 --> 00:07:15,928
Mine is just The Claude. Now, two seat
belts.

142
00:07:15,948 --> 00:07:17,678
First, set an expiry.

143
00:07:17,748 --> 00:07:22,698
I set seven days, so even if the key
leaks, it dies on its own.

144
00:07:22,748 --> 00:07:24,568
Second is to set a credit limit.

145
00:07:24,588 --> 00:07:28,518
That means even if you click the wrong
model, it physically can't charge you.

146
00:07:28,608 --> 00:07:33,088
Then create it and copy the key straight
away because OpenRouter shows it

147
00:07:33,108 --> 00:07:35,428
only once, and treat it like a password.

148
00:07:35,448 --> 00:07:38,008
Anyone who has it can use your account.

149
00:07:38,028 --> 00:07:39,478
Now, which models are free?

150
00:07:39,508 --> 00:07:43,118
Click Models at the top, and in the price
filter on the left,

151
00:07:43,248 --> 00:07:44,788
slide it all the way to free.

152
00:07:44,808 --> 00:07:47,908
Now you will only see models with free in
their name,

153
00:07:47,928 --> 00:07:50,218
and every request to those cost zero.

154
00:07:50,248 --> 00:07:51,778
And here is an important tip.

155
00:07:51,868 --> 00:07:53,708
This list changes all the time.

156
00:07:53,728 --> 00:07:56,888
Models come and go. Companies end their
free offers,

157
00:07:56,908 --> 00:07:59,188
so don't memorize the names you see today.

158
00:07:59,208 --> 00:08:02,958
Remember the filter. As long as you know
how to find the free list,

159
00:08:03,048 --> 00:08:04,888
you will always have a free model.

160
00:08:04,908 --> 00:08:07,128
Now let's connect OpenRouter to Claude
Code.

161
00:08:07,148 --> 00:08:10,558
For that, you paste a small block of
settings into your terminal,

162
00:08:10,648 --> 00:08:14,908
and the whole block is in the setup kit
for both PowerShell and command prompt.

163
00:08:14,928 --> 00:08:18,288
These settings simply tell Claude Code,
"Don't talk to Anthropic.

164
00:08:18,308 --> 00:08:22,148
Talk to OpenRouter instead, and here is my
key." Let me show you what each

165
00:08:22,168 --> 00:08:26,458
line does. The first line sends all of the
Claude Code's request to OpenRouter,

166
00:08:26,588 --> 00:08:30,188
the second line in your key, and that's
the only thing you change.

167
00:08:30,208 --> 00:08:32,128
Paste your key between these codes.

168
00:08:32,148 --> 00:08:36,828
The third line clears out the old
Anthropic key so there is no chance of
falling

169
00:08:36,848 --> 00:08:41,198
back to a paid key. And the last few lines
point Claude Code's model slots,

170
00:08:41,268 --> 00:08:45,328
Opus, Sonnet, and Haiku at
OpenRouter/free.

171
00:08:45,348 --> 00:08:48,598
OpenRouter/free is OpenRouter's free
router.

172
00:08:48,788 --> 00:08:51,348
It picks a free model for you
automatically.

173
00:08:51,368 --> 00:08:54,898
Then open your folder, right click, open
the terminal,

174
00:08:54,928 --> 00:08:57,678
paste the block, hit Enter, and type
Claude.

175
00:08:57,748 --> 00:09:01,768
One thing to know here is these settings
only apply to that terminal window,

176
00:09:01,788 --> 00:09:06,308
so next time, you paste them again or you
let Antigravity make you a shortcut.

177
00:09:06,328 --> 00:09:08,368
And this is where I almost got fooled.

178
00:09:08,388 --> 00:09:10,038
My paste failed silently.

179
00:09:10,088 --> 00:09:12,828
I typed Claude, it opened, and it looked
fine.

180
00:09:12,848 --> 00:09:15,118
Look at the top line. Spot anything wrong?

181
00:09:15,188 --> 00:09:19,248
Claude Pro? Claude Code had quietly gone
back to my paid subscription.

182
00:09:19,268 --> 00:09:22,668
If you see your Claude plan here, you're
not on a free setup.

183
00:09:22,688 --> 00:09:26,608
I opened a fresh terminal, entered the
settings one line at a time,

184
00:09:26,628 --> 00:09:31,108
typed Claude. Now it says OpenRouter/free,
API usage billing.

185
00:09:31,128 --> 00:09:34,008
That's the free one. Now let's check it's
really free.

186
00:09:34,028 --> 00:09:38,108
Type /model. You'll see Opus, Sonnet, and
Haiku,

187
00:09:38,128 --> 00:09:42,008
but those are just the names of the slots,
not what's actually answering.

188
00:09:42,028 --> 00:09:46,348
I asked it, "Which model are you?" And it
said it's a free model running through

189
00:09:46,368 --> 00:09:50,748
OpenRouter, but a model's word isn't a
proof, so let's check the bill.

190
00:09:50,768 --> 00:09:53,408
Go to OpenRouter and open the Activity
page.

191
00:09:53,428 --> 00:09:56,208
This is the real log of every request we
have sent,

192
00:09:56,228 --> 00:09:58,448
which model answered, and what it costs.

193
00:09:58,468 --> 00:10:01,648
So before I scroll down, how much do you
think this cost me?

194
00:10:01,668 --> 00:10:05,528
Three requests, about twenty-four thousand
tokens and zero dollars.

195
00:10:05,548 --> 00:10:09,488
And one more thing that might scare you,
if you type /usage in Claude Code,

196
00:10:09,508 --> 00:10:11,588
it might say something like thirteen
cents.

197
00:10:11,608 --> 00:10:16,368
Relax. That's just the Claude Code
estimating the cost at Anthropic's prices.

198
00:10:16,388 --> 00:10:19,668
The real bill is on OpenRouter, and it
says zero.

199
00:10:19,688 --> 00:10:21,168
But of course, there is a catch.

200
00:10:21,188 --> 00:10:23,348
Two, actually. First, limits.

201
00:10:23,368 --> 00:10:27,028
Free models are capped about fifty
requests a day on a new account.

202
00:10:27,048 --> 00:10:31,608
Fifty sounds like plenty until you realize
one Claude Code task can use ten

203
00:10:31,628 --> 00:10:34,888
or twenty requests because it reads files,
writes them,

204
00:10:34,908 --> 00:10:37,548
checks its own work, and fixes what it
broke.

205
00:10:37,568 --> 00:10:40,408
Hit the cap, and you'll see error four
twenty-nine.

206
00:10:40,428 --> 00:10:44,488
Four twenty-nine is just the server
saying, "You have used your free quota.

207
00:10:44,508 --> 00:10:47,197
Come back later." And the second is the
privacy.

208
00:10:47,228 --> 00:10:51,667
Unlike Ollama, your prompts and your code
now go to someone else's service,

209
00:10:51,687 --> 00:10:55,528
so don't send client code or passwords
through free Claude models.

210
00:10:55,548 --> 00:10:58,668
Keep those on Ollama. So quick question
before method three.

211
00:10:58,688 --> 00:11:02,588
Which one are you leaning towards so far,
Ollama or the OpenRouter?

212
00:11:02,608 --> 00:11:04,508
Or are you waiting for the next one?

213
00:11:04,528 --> 00:11:08,708
Tell me in the comments. I actually read
them because method three is where

214
00:11:08,728 --> 00:11:10,168
it gets really interesting.

215
00:11:10,188 --> 00:11:13,548
So here is the problem with every free
option we have seen so far.

216
00:11:13,568 --> 00:11:15,388
Every free provider has a limit.

217
00:11:15,408 --> 00:11:17,188
Google gives you some free requests.

218
00:11:17,208 --> 00:11:20,718
NVIDIA gives you some. OpenRouter gives
you fifty a day.

219
00:11:20,768 --> 00:11:25,048
On its own, each one runs out fast, but if
you put them all together,

220
00:11:25,068 --> 00:11:26,868
that's a lot of free AI, right?

221
00:11:26,888 --> 00:11:31,068
And what if when one runs out, the next
one simply takes over without you

222
00:11:31,088 --> 00:11:34,068
doing anything? That's exactly what
OmniRoute does.

223
00:11:34,088 --> 00:11:36,308
OmniRoute is a free open source app.

224
00:11:36,328 --> 00:11:39,508
You install it on your own computer, and
it sits in the middle.

225
00:11:39,528 --> 00:11:44,288
Claude Code talks only to OmniRoute, and
OmniRoute decides which AI provider

226
00:11:44,308 --> 00:11:48,438
answers each request. So Claude Code
thinks it's talking to one AI,

227
00:11:48,568 --> 00:11:51,748
but behind the scenes, OmniRoute is
juggling many,

228
00:11:51,768 --> 00:11:54,968
and we install it with one rule, and AI
does it.

229
00:11:54,988 --> 00:11:57,528
I open Antigravity and give it one prompt.

230
00:11:57,548 --> 00:12:01,708
It's in the setup kit, and it basically
says, "Check whether Claude Code is

231
00:12:01,728 --> 00:12:03,978
installed and install it if not.

232
00:12:04,008 --> 00:12:08,348
Then install OmniRoute from GitHub, start
it, and open the dashboard in my

233
00:12:08,368 --> 00:12:13,018
browser. And if any step fails, fix it and
keep going." When it's done,

234
00:12:13,128 --> 00:12:16,108
it gives me a little summary table, Claude
Code found,

235
00:12:16,128 --> 00:12:19,108
OmniRoute installed, server running,
dashboard open.

236
00:12:19,128 --> 00:12:21,828
The dashboard opens in your browser at
localhost.

237
00:12:21,848 --> 00:12:25,568
The first time, it asks for a password,
and the default one is "change me"

238
00:12:25,588 --> 00:12:29,948
literally. So log in and then go straight
to Settings and change it to something

239
00:12:29,968 --> 00:12:33,968
real. Now, the fun part here is connecting
free AI providers.

240
00:12:33,988 --> 00:12:36,078
In the dashboard, open Providers.

241
00:12:36,128 --> 00:12:39,208
You'll see a huge list. For each one, you
search for it,

242
00:12:39,228 --> 00:12:40,688
click it, and click Add.

243
00:12:40,708 --> 00:12:42,448
So number one is Kiro.

244
00:12:42,468 --> 00:12:47,088
Kiro is Amazon's AI coding app, and its
free plan includes real Claude models

245
00:12:47,108 --> 00:12:52,108
like Claude Sonnet. Search Kiro, click
Add, and OmniRoute first shows a warning.

246
00:12:52,128 --> 00:12:54,288
Read it, then pick how you want to sign
in.

247
00:12:54,308 --> 00:12:56,328
It gives you a short code and a link.

248
00:12:56,348 --> 00:12:59,148
Open the link, sign in, check that the
code matches,

249
00:12:59,168 --> 00:13:02,998
and approve. It says device authorized,
and back in OmniRoute,

250
00:13:03,088 --> 00:13:06,308
Kiro shows connected. And two honest
things about Kiro,

251
00:13:06,328 --> 00:13:09,468
the free allowance is small, so it won't
last long.

252
00:13:09,488 --> 00:13:13,108
And during sign-up, it even opened a
subscription required page,

253
00:13:13,128 --> 00:13:18,228
and OmniRoute itself marks Kiro as terms
of service avoid because using it

254
00:13:18,248 --> 00:13:22,608
this way may go against Kiro's rules, so
this one is your account and your

255
00:13:22,628 --> 00:13:27,178
call. So number two is Antigravity, the
same Google app we have been using,

256
00:13:27,228 --> 00:13:28,828
but this time as a provider.

257
00:13:28,848 --> 00:13:31,418
Click Add. It opens a Google sign-in.

258
00:13:31,448 --> 00:13:32,948
Pick your account and allow.

259
00:13:32,968 --> 00:13:35,798
Then your browser lands on a page that
doesn't load,

260
00:13:35,948 --> 00:13:39,948
and that's normal. Copy the full address
from the top of that page and paste

261
00:13:39,968 --> 00:13:42,348
it back into the OmniRoute and click
Connect.

262
00:13:42,368 --> 00:13:46,388
Now you get Gemini model plus some Claude
models within limits.

263
00:13:46,408 --> 00:13:48,428
The warning here is even stronger.

264
00:13:48,448 --> 00:13:51,248
OmniRoute says Google may restrict the
account,

265
00:13:51,268 --> 00:13:54,548
so please use a spare account, not your
main one.

266
00:13:54,568 --> 00:13:56,328
So number three is NVIDIA.

267
00:13:56,348 --> 00:14:01,208
NVIDIA, the graphics card company, also
gives free access to bunch of AI models

268
00:14:01,228 --> 00:14:03,068
on build.nvidia.com.

269
00:14:03,088 --> 00:14:07,388
Create a free account, go to API keys, and
click Generate API key.

270
00:14:07,408 --> 00:14:08,988
Give it a name and copy it.

271
00:14:09,008 --> 00:14:12,148
Then back in OmniRoute, open NVIDIA, click
Add,

272
00:14:12,168 --> 00:14:16,648
and paste the key. And here, switch on
Import only free models so only the

273
00:14:16,668 --> 00:14:18,828
free ones comes in, and then Save.

274
00:14:18,848 --> 00:14:20,478
Number four is Gemini.

275
00:14:20,512 --> 00:14:23,212
And Google AI Studio gives you a free key
for it.

276
00:14:23,232 --> 00:14:28,411
Go to Google AI Studio, click Get API key
and create API key and copy it.

277
00:14:28,431 --> 00:14:31,892
In OmniRoute, open Gemini, click Add and
paste it.

278
00:14:31,912 --> 00:14:34,572
Hit Check to make sure it works, and then
Save.

279
00:14:34,592 --> 00:14:36,572
And that's four providers connected.

280
00:14:36,592 --> 00:14:40,552
Now, one by one, they are small, so let's
make them work as one team.

281
00:14:40,572 --> 00:14:43,252
In OmniRoute, the team is called combo.

282
00:14:43,272 --> 00:14:45,782
A combo is a list of models in an order.

283
00:14:45,872 --> 00:14:49,712
The first one answers, and if it's busy,
out of free credits,

284
00:14:49,732 --> 00:14:52,451
or failing, the next one takes over
automatically.

285
00:14:52,471 --> 00:14:55,632
Go to Combos, click Create, and give it a
name.

286
00:14:55,652 --> 00:14:59,512
I called mine Claude, and you'll see why
that name matters in a second.

287
00:14:59,532 --> 00:15:02,852
Now, add the steps. For each step, you
pick a provider,

288
00:15:02,872 --> 00:15:05,932
then a model, and then leave the account
on automatic.

289
00:15:05,952 --> 00:15:10,162
Click Add Step. I put Gemini first, then
Kiro's Claude Sonnet,

290
00:15:10,332 --> 00:15:12,512
and then more free backups behind them.

291
00:15:12,532 --> 00:15:15,872
You can use the little up and down arrows
to change the order.

292
00:15:15,892 --> 00:15:18,712
Top goes first. Then it asks for the
strategy,

293
00:15:18,732 --> 00:15:23,112
which is simply how OmniRoute chooses
between the models in your combo.

294
00:15:23,132 --> 00:15:27,432
I picked Smart Routing, so it scores each
option and picks the best one.

295
00:15:27,452 --> 00:15:29,572
Then Save, and the combo is ready.

296
00:15:29,592 --> 00:15:33,262
Now, Claude Code needs one key to use this
whole combo,

297
00:15:33,412 --> 00:15:35,532
and that's the OmniRoute API key.

298
00:15:35,552 --> 00:15:37,452
It's OmniRoute's own password.

299
00:15:37,472 --> 00:15:41,712
Claude Code shows this key to OmniRoute,
and OmniRoute then uses all your

300
00:15:41,732 --> 00:15:43,872
provider keys behind the scenes.

301
00:15:43,892 --> 00:15:47,472
In the dashboard, open API Keys and click
Create API Key.

302
00:15:47,492 --> 00:15:50,812
Give it a name, like Claude Code, and then
create it.

303
00:15:50,832 --> 00:15:54,482
And now copy it straight away because just
like the other keys,

304
00:15:54,612 --> 00:15:56,432
you only get to see it once.

305
00:15:56,452 --> 00:15:59,452
Now, I don't want to type commands every
time I use this,

306
00:15:59,472 --> 00:16:01,852
so I give Antigravity one more prompt.

307
00:16:01,872 --> 00:16:04,752
It says, "Make two shortcuts on my
desktop.

308
00:16:04,772 --> 00:16:09,072
The first is Free Claude Code," and
launches Claude Code through OmniRoute

309
00:16:09,092 --> 00:16:12,852
using my combo. The second shortcut is
OmniRoute Dashboard,

310
00:16:12,872 --> 00:16:14,532
which opens the control panel.

311
00:16:14,552 --> 00:16:19,372
And one important safety tip here is
Antigravity will ask for your OmniRoute

312
00:16:19,392 --> 00:16:21,392
key while it sets this up.

313
00:16:21,412 --> 00:16:23,052
Don't paste the key into the chat.

314
00:16:23,072 --> 00:16:26,032
Ask it to open a file, paste the key into
the file,

315
00:16:26,052 --> 00:16:27,732
save it, and type Continue.

316
00:16:27,752 --> 00:16:30,672
That way, your key never sits in a chat
history.

317
00:16:30,692 --> 00:16:32,152
And then one last fix.

318
00:16:32,172 --> 00:16:36,552
When Claude Code connects to OmniRoute,
its model menu can show every model

319
00:16:36,572 --> 00:16:38,992
from your providers, including paid ones.

320
00:16:39,012 --> 00:16:41,852
Pick a paid one by accident, and you get
errors or,

321
00:16:41,872 --> 00:16:45,192
worse, a bill. So I give Antigravity one
more prompt,

322
00:16:45,212 --> 00:16:50,272
"Turn on hide paid models in OmniRoute and
keep my Claude combo as the default."

323
00:16:50,292 --> 00:16:51,852
That's exactly what happened.

324
00:16:51,872 --> 00:16:54,172
The menu only showed free options.

325
00:16:54,192 --> 00:16:56,652
Everything's connected. Moment of truth.

326
00:16:56,672 --> 00:16:58,512
I double-click Free Claude Code.

327
00:16:58,532 --> 00:17:02,032
It checks OmniRoute. It's running, and
Claude Code opens.

328
00:17:02,052 --> 00:17:05,622
So obvious question is, "Which model are
you?" And it answers,

329
00:17:05,692 --> 00:17:09,452
"I am Claude." But remember, a model's
word isn't a proof,

330
00:17:09,472 --> 00:17:12,372
so let's check the bill. I open the
OmniRoute dashboard,

331
00:17:12,392 --> 00:17:15,072
go to Logs, which is the list of every
request,

332
00:17:15,092 --> 00:17:16,872
and look at this line.

333
00:17:16,892 --> 00:17:19,252
"Model: Claude Sonnet four point five.

334
00:17:19,272 --> 00:17:21,732
Provider: Kiro. Combo: Claude.

335
00:17:21,752 --> 00:17:24,852
Status: two hundred." And two hundred just
means success.

336
00:17:24,872 --> 00:17:29,572
So that's real Claude Sonnet answering
inside Claude Code through a free account.

337
00:17:29,592 --> 00:17:31,972
That's the receipt. But a hi proves
nothing,

338
00:17:31,992 --> 00:17:33,512
so let's build something real.

339
00:17:33,532 --> 00:17:37,752
On my machine, I asked, "Design me a
simple personal note-taking application,"

340
00:17:37,772 --> 00:17:39,752
and it doesn't just start writing code.

341
00:17:39,772 --> 00:17:43,952
It's in plan mode, a Claude Code mode
where it plans and asks you questions

342
00:17:43,972 --> 00:17:45,412
before it writes anything.

343
00:17:45,432 --> 00:17:48,492
So it asked me which tech stack, which
features,

344
00:17:48,512 --> 00:17:50,192
and how should the notes be stored.

345
00:17:50,212 --> 00:17:53,252
Then it showed me a plan and only then
starts building.

346
00:17:53,272 --> 00:17:54,632
And here is the coolest part.

347
00:17:54,652 --> 00:17:58,912
Straight from the logs, real Claude did
the planning until its free limit

348
00:17:58,932 --> 00:18:02,652
ran out. The OmniRoute switched by itself
to free NVIDIA,

349
00:18:02,672 --> 00:18:05,072
and it kept building. I didn't touch
anything.

350
00:18:05,092 --> 00:18:06,832
That's the whole point of a combo.

351
00:18:06,852 --> 00:18:09,452
Now, honestly, the first version didn't
work.

352
00:18:09,472 --> 00:18:13,892
It took a few fix rounds and a final
cleanup, but here it is,

353
00:18:13,912 --> 00:18:18,312
notes, tags, markdown, search, and it even
survives a refresh.

354
00:18:18,332 --> 00:18:20,932
And the bill for all of that is zero
dollars.

355
00:18:20,952 --> 00:18:22,852
And here is your free Claude Code map.

356
00:18:22,872 --> 00:18:26,832
Which one fits you? Privacy or offline, go
with Ollama.

357
00:18:26,852 --> 00:18:29,962
If you don't have strong hardware and you
want speed,

358
00:18:30,132 --> 00:18:34,212
go with OpenRouter. And if you want it to
keep going when one provider runs

359
00:18:34,232 --> 00:18:36,612
out, the best option is OmniRoute.

360
00:18:36,632 --> 00:18:39,571
And the four rules. One, nothing is
unlimited.

361
00:18:39,591 --> 00:18:42,472
And the two, cloud providers see what you
send,

362
00:18:42,492 --> 00:18:44,612
so keep private code on Ollama.

363
00:18:44,632 --> 00:18:48,932
And the third thing is Kiro and
Antigravity may go against their terms,

364
00:18:48,952 --> 00:18:52,392
so it's your call. And the fourth one is
things will break.

365
00:18:52,412 --> 00:18:56,022
Copy, paste, fix this, and always check
the bill,

366
00:18:56,072 --> 00:18:59,972
not the label. Everyone told you Claude
Code costs twenty dollars a month.

367
00:18:59,992 --> 00:19:03,302
Today, we ran the real Claude Code three
ways for free,

368
00:19:03,392 --> 00:19:07,552
offline, in the cloud, and with the
providers that switch by themself.

369
00:19:07,572 --> 00:19:09,372
Same Claude Code, zero bill.

370
00:19:09,392 --> 00:19:12,492
The free setup kit with the model list,
every prompt,

371
00:19:12,512 --> 00:19:15,472
and every setting is in the first link in
the description.

372
00:19:15,492 --> 00:19:17,932
Tell me in the comments which one you are
trying first.

373
00:19:17,952 --> 00:19:21,952
And if this save you twenty dollars a
month, subscribe to AI with Kalyan.

374
00:19:21,972 --> 00:19:24,532
I test these AI tools so you don't have
to.

375
00:19:24,552 --> 00:19:27,242
And if you want to see what else Claude
Code can do,

376
00:19:27,452 --> 00:19:29,972
this video right here is one to watch
next.

377
00:19:29,992 --> 00:19:35,712
Every week on AI with Kalyan, one AI tool
